Z||000000||Bill Osmer||10-25-1999||01:41 PM||||The lock cylinder on my engine compartment was drilled out by (previous owner?) There was a previous post somewhere that identified three (3) diff. lock cyl. P/Ns, one was for my 86 GT (P/N 20496717). I ordered that number from my local Pontiac dealer - they provided Pontiac's replacement number (12507464). It's completely wrong! (i.e., does not fit) I had them double check the number, which they did and came up with the same. Their solution: bring it to the service department to have someone knowledgeable to fix it!! I've already removed the lock and cylinder mechanisms (rusted screws and all), so I've already done their WORK. <P>Does anyone know of a more correct procedure (or P/N) to let me fix this?? The only other that I can think of is to find a donor, which I'll still have to get re-keyed.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Linx||10-26-1999||08:43 AM||||You may be trying too hard.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I bought my lock set at Advance auto parts.  I've always assumed they were common cylinders; they were in bubble packs and seemed to be used on lots of different vehicles.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 10-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||10-26-1999||09:35 AM||||This is true....Most of the Auto parts stores have a section devoted to replacement lock cylinders.  I would check there first.  The parts reference they have may be accurate as well.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-26-1999||01:52 PM||||the most important thing to remember when shopping for any Fiero part is that nearly everything mechanical on these cars is used in other GM product. That's why it always @#%@#$%@ when some dink says they can't fix it because it's a fiero..... Mechanically it's pretty much the same as most other GM's of the time.||||reg||1
